Annex 2 – Funded Activities

1. Background and purpose of the Grant

1.1. England’s trees are facing increasing threats from pests and disease. Healthy trees help tackle the impacts of climate change, provide wildlife habitats, clean the air we breathe and produce the food we eat. To reduce the impact of tree pests and diseases, the Government is designing a future tree health scheme that will replace and expand the existing Countryside Stewardship Woodland Tree Health grants. The future scheme will aim to build the resilience of England’s trees, woods and forests, mitigating and minimising the impact of pests and diseases, improving the capacity of our trees to adapt to changing pressures and enhancing the benefits trees provide for future generations.

1.2. The Tree Health Pilot will expand on support available through the existing Countryside Stewardship Woodland Tree Health grant by testing new ways to help people who own or manage trees, to keep trees healthy by stopping the spread of pests and diseases and maintaining the treescape through resilient restocking after pest and diseases induced tree loss. Learning generated through the pilot will be used to develop future incentives schemes for tree health.

1.3. The Tree Health pilot is funded through Defra’s Farming and Countryside Programme and delivered in partnership with the FC. The Tree Health Pilot delivers on the Agricultural Transition Plan 2020, supporting the commitment to the 25 Year Environment Plan, the Tree Health Resilience Strategy and the UK’s carbon net zero goals.

The Oak Processionary Moth (OPM) grant aims to test and improve ways of managing the risks of OPM, identifying and protecting the value of oak trees within the OPM Established Area and controlling the spread of OPM in oak trees. The results of the pilot will help develop the future policy for tree health schemes.

1.4. Specifically, grants are available to Local Authorities to help plan OPM management. The fund will pay for the creation of an OPM Management Plan and Communications Strategy covering all of the infected or at risk oak trees, the surveying of OPM for those trees,’communications collateral’ (i.e. leaflets and flyers), biosecurity kits and biosecurity training.

1.5. Specifically, the OPM Management Plan will require the Local Authority to identify an eligible geographical area of OPM establishment (“Established Area”) to be approved by the FC and form a plan to manage OPM risks and preserve the integrity and value of the oak trees. In the supporting Communications Strategy, the Local Authority will need to identify key stakeholders with oak trees in high-risk areas to work collaboratively with, and outline actions to raise public awareness of OPM through targeted communications and engagement.

2. Funded Activities

2.1. Funded Activities and items for which Grant funding is payable include:

the creation of an OPM Management Plan for OPM affected trees. This will be paid as a standard cost and a minimum of £1000 for applications including up to 100 trees. For applications including over 100 trees there will be a scaled increase in grant payment of £10 per tree.

the hiring of contractors to carry out OPM Surveys of the trees subject of the Grant application to identify OPM, for which a minimum of two independent and valid quotes for the work must be obtained by the Grant Recipient and submitted to the Authority, and the Authority in its sole discretion will select the most appropriate quote (a 100% Intervention Rate will apply to surveying activities)

‘comms collateral’ (i.e. signage and leaflets) at a standard cost of £300.

biosecurity training at a standard cost of £40.

Funding to purchase the following biosecurity capital items is also available:

long-handled, stiff bristled brush

boot pick

container/bucket big enough to fit a boot in

Defra approved disinfectant

tape

posts

This is a fixed, standard cost package of £30. In order to claim for this package, the Grant Recipient will be required to submit photographic evidence of the items listed above with their THP OPM claim form.

3. OPM management plan and communications strategy

3.1. The OPM Management Plan will require the Local Authority to identify an eligible geographical area of OPM establishment (“Established Area”) to be approved by the FC and plan to manage OPM risks and preserve the integrity and value of the oak trees.

3.2. The OPM Management Plan should identify the value of the oak trees under threat due to OPM. These values should include social, economic, and environmental values.

3.3. The OPM Management Plan should address the context of the pest, identify the ways in which OPM poses risks and set out actions to monitor and manage the risks associated with OPM.

3.4. Where the OPM Management Plan identifies protected environmental features that may be directly impacted by OPM, for example protected species on designated sites, the Local Authority must consult with the relevant organisation.

3.5 The Plan should identify key stakeholders with oak trees in high-risk areas to work collaboratively with. It should outline actions to raise public awareness of OPM in the local area through a targeted communications strategy and engagement activity.

4. OPM Survey

4.1. OPM Surveys will only be funded for Land in the Established Area.

4.2. For surveys to be completed by a contractor, the Local Authority will need to procure a competent surveyor, who can evidence value for money. This can usually be proven by gathering at least two quotes, which must be evidenced when submitting an OPM application. Contractors should have the skills and equipment to undertake the surveying effectively and safely. The Arboricultural Association provides further information on best practice.

4.3. The OPM Surveys must cover the oak trees within the Established Area.

4.4. OPM Survey results must be recorded in line with FC requirements detailed in the THP guidance and submitted by the OPM Grant Agreement Holder to the FC.

